<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 http://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
    <parent>
        <artifactId>parallel-consumer-proxy-clients</artifactId>
        <groupId>bz.stub.parallelconsumer</groupId>
        <version>0.6.0.0-SNAPSHOT</version>
    </parent>
    <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

    <name>Parallel Consumer - Proxy Client Go</name>
    <artifactId>parallel-consumer-proxy-client-go</artifactId>
    <packaging>pom</packaging>

    <!--
        Thin Maven wrapper over the Go toolchain (go.mod beside this file). The commands below run
        ONLY under -Dpc.foreignClients - see the foreign-clients profile in the clients aggregator
        for why opt-in is load-bearing - and both go through bin/foreign-client-step.sh, which
        decides what an ABSENT go means: a banner and a skip by default, red under
        -Dpc.foreign.strict=1, which is what the CI row passes. The commands themselves are declared
        here and nowhere else (astubbs#242).

        THE TEST NEEDS THE SIDECAR, and the go-sidecar-harness profile below is what gets it. One Go
        test spawns parallel-consumer-proxy's real Main over the real wire, and with no Maven
        dependency at all `-am` did not pull the proxy into the reactor - so the CI row's own
        command, `./mvnw test -pl :parallel-consumer-proxy-client-go -am -Dpc.foreignClients`, ran
        the Go test against an unbuilt `parallel-consumer-proxy/target` and failed there. The profile
        declares that dependency in the opt-in lane only and writes the resolved classpath where the
        test reads it.

        The Go test still fails rather than skipping when the sidecar is absent, because a test that
        quietly does not run is not a passing test.

        TWO FLAGS ON THE TEST COMMAND, AND NEITHER IS DECORATION.

        -count=1 DEFEATS GO'S TEST CACHE, and it is here because the cache produced a false pass that
        was caught by accident. `go test` caches a package's result keyed on its own inputs, and the
        sidecar this module spawns is not one of them - so breaking the sidecar's refusal on purpose,
        to prove the handshake test could fail, returned `ok (cached)` and the arm read as green.
        A CI row is a fresh checkout and never sees it; a developer changing the engine and re-running
        the Go client does, silently, which is the worse half.

        -race is the Go client's own concurrency check, and it is the cheapest real win available on
        the whole polyglot surface: this is the client with the executor fan-out, the detector is
        built into the toolchain, and it costs one flag.
        docs/inflight/static-polyglot-client-analysers.md ranked it first of five and recorded that
        the gating recipe did not use it while a per-language note claimed it did.
    -->
    <properties>
        <pc.foreign.skip>false</pc.foreign.skip>
        <pc.foreign.build.executable>bash</pc.foreign.build.executable>
        <pc.foreign.build.args>${pc.foreign.step} --tool go -- go build ./...</pc.foreign.build.args>
        <pc.foreign.test.executable>bash</pc.foreign.test.executable>
        <pc.foreign.test.args>${pc.foreign.step} --tool go -- go test -race -count=1 ./...</pc.foreign.test.args>
    </properties>

    <profiles>
        <!--
            The sidecar this module's handshake test spawns. The clients aggregator owns the wiring
            and the reasoning - its `sidecar-classpath` execution, and why hoisting it is safe; what
            is here is the one thing that cannot be hoisted, the dependency that makes `-am` build
            parallel-consumer-proxy before this module's suite tries to spawn it. Test-scoped and
            inside the opt-in profile, so an ordinary build has no edge to the sidecar at all.
        -->
        <profile>
            <id>go-sidecar-harness</id>
            <activation>
                <property>
                    <name>pc.foreignClients</name>
                </property>
            </activation>
            <dependencies>
                <dependency>
                    <groupId>bz.stub.parallelconsumer</groupId>
                    <artifactId>parallel-consumer-proxy</artifactId>
                    <version>${project.version}</version>
                    <scope>test</scope>
                </dependency>
            </dependencies>
            <build>
                <plugins>
                    <plugin>
                        <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
                        <artifactId>maven-dependency-plugin</artifactId>
                    </plugin>
                </plugins>
            </build>
        </profile>
    </profiles>

    <build>
        <plugins>
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.codehaus.mojo</groupId>
                <artifactId>exec-maven-plugin</artifactId>
            </plugin>
            <!--
                NO maven-clean-plugin FILESET HERE, AND THAT IS A MEASURED RESULT RATHER THAN AN
                OMISSION - the note exists because "nothing to clean" and "nobody wired the clean"
                look identical in a pom (astubbs#242).

                GO WRITES NOTHING INTO THIS DIRECTORY. `go build ./...` matches several packages, and
                for that form the toolchain compiles as a check and DISCARDS the objects - verified
                by running it in a pristine tree and finding not one new file, tracked or ignored.
                What it produces instead goes to the build cache, GOCACHE (~/.cache/go-build), with
                the modules in GOMODCACHE (~/go/pkg/mod). Both are outside the module, both are
                shared with every other checkout and agent on the machine, and both are this
                language's ~/.m2 - so `go clean -cache` or `-modcache` is exactly the deletion this
                repo forbids, not the one it wants. `go vet` and `go tool staticcheck` are the same
                story.

                THE ONE BUILT ARTEFACT IS ALREADY COVERED. The shared conformance suite prebuilds
                `go build -o target/conformance-runner ./cmd/conformance-runner` - grep
                `target/conformance-runner` in the conformance module's LanguageRunners - and Maven's
                default clean takes target/ whole, no configuration needed. Proven both ways: the
                binary is there after the suite's prebuild and gone after `mvn clean`.

                gen/ IS SOURCE, NOT OUTPUT. The protobuf stubs under gen/ are committed on purpose,
                because Go has no codegen step at `go get` time - scripts/generate-proto.sh says so
                in its header. Deleting them would break a consumer of this module, and the
                regeneration check is `git status` staying clean, not a clean removing them.

                So if a future change here starts emitting into the module - a `go build -o` landing
                outside target/, a `go test -c` binary - it needs a fileset added below, and this
                paragraph is the reason nobody had to write one before.
            -->
        </plugins>
    </build>

</project>
